ld be 3? \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science)\" - Logging woven into science sounds like this. Hmm.\n\nThe anchored description for 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). The proposal says embed as core architecture constraint, mandatory review. That is logging woven into science. Might be 3.\n\nLet's choose 2? Let's think: paradigm breaking requires changes relationship AND addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It doesn't address larger problem. Paradigm stretching changes relationship. Embedding logging into architecture changes relationship from afterthought to core design. Could be 3.\n\nI think 2 is slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Actually it does treat compliance as logging practice, just earlier.\n\nThe instrument adapted. I'll go with 3? Hmm.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 = interesting 1 = common mundane boring\n\nEmbedding logging as architecture constraint is fairly common compliance best practice, maybe mundane. Might be common. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting.\n\nProbably common suggestion to bake compliance in. Not rare. So originality 1 or 2.\n\nGiven typical lab proposals, this is fairly standard \"shift left\" compliance. I'd rate originality 1 = common, mundane.\n\nEvidence: Treating logging as a mandatory architectural layer and review gate is a common, mundane compliance best practice.\n\nScore 1.\n\nLet's output in required format.\n\nNeed for each subscale: first write one sentence evidence then give its score as integer.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nFormat likely JSON? Probably plain text with keys.\n\nInstructions: For each subscale (applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptabi