        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P2288.\n\nProposal: Logging-Spec Gate Before Code Freeze. Mandatory technical-design checkpoint where logging specification reviewed signed off before prototype enters build phase. Add half-day peer-review session to existing design-review calendar using one-page checklist template derived from Article 12 paragraphs 2 and 3; no project advances past spec without logged compliance sign-off in ticket tracker. It front-loads architectural decisions most expensive to retrofit...\n\nRate each subscale.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nIs this common? Gate before code freeze is fairly standard design review practice. Logging spec gate is maybe unusual but not ingenious. Might be unusual, interesting; shows some imagination -> 3? Or interesting =2? Common mundane? Many labs have compliance gates. This is somewhat standard. Could be common, mundane boring ->1? Hmm.\n\nThe reference class is responses an experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expected response: add logging requirements, checklist. Might be common. Originality: probably interesting but not rare ingenious. I'd say 2 = interesting. Or 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Let's think: Logging-spec gate before code freeze is a process improvement. Is it rare? Many labs have design gates. Adding logging spec might be common. I'd lean 2.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal adds checkpoint, checklist, front-load decisions. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements tools/processes but not changing relationship fundamentally. So slightly paradigm stretching =2.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult...\nAdding half-day peer-review session to existing calendar using one-page checklist. Seems easy low cost. So 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nThis is standard compliance gate. Acceptable. 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nWill logging spec gate ensure meeting logging mandate? It front-loads architectural decisions, eliminates rework. Likely contributes but does not guarantee logs are technically capable per Article 12 paragraph 3 specifics like automatic recording. Might help but not fully solve. Could be partial solution. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\nThe problem is how to meet logging mandate / adapt R&D. This is directly about meeting logging mandate via design gate. So 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating before number.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Might be plain lines.\n\nInstruction: Return rating of this proposal