        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\nWe need rating for proposal P2818.\n\nProposal: Tie Grant Disbursements to Compliance Readiness Gates. Restructure internal funding so project budgets release in tranches gated by verified logging integration and technical-documentation completeness rather than solely on scientific milestones... Add two compliance checkpoints... into existing grant-disbursement workflow using project-management tooling we already operate, requiring no new software procurement. Ensures scarce public/private R&D funds flow only toward systems that can demonstrate Article 12 conformity...\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes tying grant disbursements to compliance readiness gates solve logging mandate? It ensures funds flow only toward systems that can demonstrate Article 12 conformity, reduces risk of stranded non-compliant assets. It contributes to solution but does not directly implement logging; it incentivizes. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Score 3? Could be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does gating solve logging mandate? It ensures compliance readiness but doesn't itself record logs. It is indirect. Probably contributes. So effectiveness =3.\n\nEvidence sentence: The gating mechanism aligns funding with verified logging integration but does not itself build the logging capability required by Article 12.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolv