ore 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable. Assigning and recording identity of verifier, data protection implications briefing. Might be somewhat uncommon? Common strategies that violate no norms? Recording identity for audit is common compliance. Legal/HR jointly draft, brief on data protection. Probably common strategies that violate no norms. Score 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious... Reference class set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Verifier attribution protocol is pretty standard compliance. Likely common, mundane, boring. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. This is standard compliance practice. Score 1.\n\nCheck definitions: paradigm_relatedness 4 = paradigm breaking... 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nYes.\n\nNeed output format: rating of proposal echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then <subscale> (integer). Order listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide perhaps JSON? Probably plain lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1321\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure each evidence sentence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
