le. 2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible. 1 totally infeasible.\n\nStand up board requires engineers + legal counsel time quarterly, charter, rubric, remediation sprint. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Maybe 3 = some changes or reasonably feasible measures. Yes.\n\nacceptability: 4 common strategies violate no norms. 3 somewhat uncommon unusual do not offend sensibilities. 2 offends somewhat... 1 radically violates...\n\nInternal audit board is common, acceptable. So 4.\n\napplicability: 4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem: lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal does quarterly audit to ensure logging meets Article12(2). That's directly related. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps partial). Does audit board guarantee logs technically allow automatic recording? It ensures logging compliance but does not implement technical logging capability itself. It helps ensure compliance but does not create logs. Might be partial solution. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Audit helps meet mandate but doesn't build logging. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). Could argue 4? It would solve traceability? Hmm.\n\nThe logging mandate requires technical capability. Board reviews logs, flags gaps, triggers remediation. That contributes to meeting mandate but doesn't implement logs. So partial.\n\nThus effectiveness = 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably each on separate line.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably just lines.\n\nInstruction: Return the rating of this proposal, ec