ommon practice. So originality = 1.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nProposal measures overhead cost of full Article 12 logging, establishing budget before architects lock designs. That directly addresses meeting logging mandate while preserving performance/SLA. So applies directly. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: It measures CPU/memory/latency cost of Article 12 logs and sets budgets for production designs.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\nBenchmarking overhead gives numbers but does not actually implement logging. It informs design but doesn't guarantee compliance. It contributes to solution, partial. It won't solve mandate alone. So 3.\n\nEvidence: Knowing overhead helps architects respect SLAs but does not by itself create the required automatic logging or traceability.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Benchmarking is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\nIt requires running shared middleware across three workloads for two sprints, recording latency deltas... That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? It's a benchmarking effort. Maybe 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 4 = easy to implement at low cost? Two sprints with production-scale data volumes maybe costly. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: It needs two sprints of production-scale testing and cross-team coordination, which is feasible but not trivially low-cost.\n\nReturn rating ech