m.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is introducing tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nIt addresses logging mandate. Yes directly. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill capture Article 12 events... It seems will solve logging mandate. Probably 4? Might be partial because only prototype logging? But it's about meet logging mandate. Reasonable and will solve stated problem. I'd say 3? The proposal says zero-effort for engineer removing iteration-velocity risk. It captures timestamps, model calls, input-output hashes. Might be enough. Could be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. I think 3 = contributes to solution but partial? Actually logging mandate is met. Maybe 4.\n\nCheck anchored: effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Seems yes.\n\nThus scores:\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 3\noriginality 2\nparadigm_relatedness 2\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 4? Or 3?\n\nPossibly effectiveness 3 because logs must be for high-risk systems only, prot