ess: degree preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nThis is introducing log-spec template and workshop but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That is level 2. Could be 3? It changes relationship? Logging woven into science? Maybe not. I'd go 2.\n\neffectiveness: degree idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps partial)\nProposal ensures logging design technically viable and prevents re-architecting, meets mandate. Seems will solve problem of how to meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. Might be 4? It solves logging mandate compliance by defining what to record upfront. Could solve main problem. Probably 4.\n\napplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y part of stated problem)\nProposal does design sprint to meet Article 12 logging mandate. That's direct. So 4.\n\nNow format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProvide output.\n\nProbably JSON? Instructions say return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be plain lines.\n\nI'll output structured:\n\nid: P2523\nacceptability_evidence: ...