ab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nShared microservice introduces new tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching =2. Could be 1? It changes process somewhat. I'd say 2.\n\nEffectiveness: 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). 2 unreasonable unlikely. 1 solves unrelated.\n\nWill shared logging microservice meet logging mandate? It enables automatic recording, centralizes logs, reduces cost. Seems will solve problem of meeting logging mandate and adapting R&D. Reasonable and will solve stated problem? Possibly yes. Might be partial because need per-system specifics like Article12(3) specific data points. But shared service can capture events. I think 4 = will solve stated problem without regard workability. Or 3 contributes? I'd argue it solves core logging mandate. So 4.\n\nApplicability: 4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). The stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Shared service does exactly that. So 4.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). Probably output list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1701\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nImplementability evidence: The proposal requires a dedicated platform team for one quarter to build an open-source log store and SDK, which is reasonably feasible with existing engineering resources. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Centralized logging with role-scoped access is a common compliance engineering practice that violates no legal or social norms. Score 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Shared microservices for logging are standard DevOps practice and not rare or ingenious in AI labs. Score 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a shared tooling layer but still treats Article 12 as a logging practice rather than changing how research is conducted. Score 2.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: A centralized event-capture service with an SDK can provide automatic lifetime logging and traceability required by Article 12 for all high-risk systems. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: Building a reusable logging platform directly addresses the mandate to meet record-keeping and reduce per-project compliance cost. Score 4.\n\nCheck ordering.\n\nProvide output.\n\n",
