mpliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance\n\nHere proposal turns compliance into sales document, partners compliance office with product marketing, creates certification brief. That's changing relationship: compliance becomes market offering. So paradigm stretching? It changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - compliance becomes differentiator. Might be paradigm stretching = 3. Not breaking because doesn't change what lab researches fundamentally. It's new partners/offerings born from compliance. So 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources...\nProposal audits systems against Article 12(3) minimum logging fields, package verified logging capability... Does it solve identified problem directly related? It addresses meeting logging mandate via audit, and adapts R&D via marketing. Solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Probably 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\nReasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Auditing and marketing doesn't guarantee logging implementation. It assumes audit leads to compliance? Might be partial solution: it ensures audit but does not guarantee technical logging implementation. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial). Because meeting logging mandate requires actual technical implementation, not just documentation/marketing. Audit may identify gaps but doesn't implement logs. Hmm proposal says audit all existing and in-development systems against minimum logging fields then package... Could be that audit triggers implementation? Not explicit. It says we audit then package verified logging capability. Implies capability exists. Might be partial.\n\nCould be 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability: if you could do it, it would solve main problem? Might need actual logs. I'd say 3.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nAudit systems against Article 12(3) minimum logging fields, produce certification brief per system, partner compliance with marketing. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Audit is work. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities = 4. Marketing compliance is common. No violation. So 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each subscale before number.\n\nFormat: return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON output? It says give... Provide lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2516\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order listed: originality, paradigm_relatedness, applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft.\n\noriginality_evidence: The proposal repackages mandatory logging audits as a marketable traceability certification, which is unusual among expected lab responses focused purely on technical compliance. => originality 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It shifts compliance from a cost center to a procurement-facing asset by pairing the compliance office with product marketing. => paradigm_relatedness 3\n\napplicability_evidence: Auditing systems against Article 12(3) fields and mapping logs to Article 12(2)(a)-(c) directly addresses how the lab meets the logging mandate. => applicability 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The audit and certification help ensure traceabil