d problem)\nIt does X to get Y: schema -> compliance. Clearly directly related. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The schema maps Article 12(3) minimum logging items to concrete engineering fields for the lab’s high-risk systems.\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nOne Dev Engineer drafts schema in one week; wire into serving path during next integration sprint. Seems reasonably feasible, some changes. Might be 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Wiring into serving path of production biometric verification pipelines may be moderate change. Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: A single engineer can draft the schema in one week and integrate it in an existing sprint, indicating modest engineering effort.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nThis is standard compliance. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Creating a compliant log schema aligns with legal obligations and industry norms for auditability.\n\nNovelty originality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nProposal is fixed event-data schema for biometric systems. Is that common, mundane? Probably common practice: defining logging schema. Not surprising. Might be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting? Hmm.\n\nOriginality level 4 requires not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative or surprising. No. Level 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination. This is standard compliance engineering. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Defining typed log fields for regulatory minimums is routine compliance engineering rather than an imaginative approach.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes rel